This function takes a sorted list `arr` and a `target` value as input. It initializes two pointers, `left` and `right`, to the start and end of the list, respectively. The function then enters a loop that continues as long as `left` is less than or equal to `right`. In each iteration, it calculates the middle index `mid` and checks if the value at `mid` is equal to the `target`. If it is, the function returns `mid`. If the value at `mid` is less than the `target`, the function updates `left` to `mid + 1`. Otherwise, it updates `right` to `mid - 1`. If the loop completes without finding the `target`, the function returns `-1`.

This function follows the standard binary search algorithm and works efficiently for sorted lists. It has a time complexity of O(log n), where n is the length of the list.

